<p>After a hailstorm or a violent burst of wind, one of the first questions an insurance adjuster may ask is simple: <em>did a storm actually hit this property on that date?</em> NOAA weather records are one of the most useful tools for answering it. Used correctly, they can strengthen a claim and help prevent disputes over the date of loss. Used carelessly, they can create confusion.</p>

<p>This guide explains which NOAA weather records exist, how to find reports for McAllen and the rest of Hidalgo County, how adjusters use the data, and where the limits are. We include real examples from the Rio Grande Valley (RGV) so you can see what these records look like in practice.</p>

<h2>The main NOAA weather records for property damage</h2>

<p>"NOAA data" is not one thing. Several agencies and products under NOAA collect storm reports, and each has a different purpose and timeline.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What it contains</th><th>Timing</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI Storm Events Database</a></td><td>Official record of significant weather events by county or forecast zone, with dates, times, magnitudes (hail size, wind speed), locations, and narratives. Records go back to 1950.</td><td>Updated monthly; new months typically published after a delay of a few months.</td><td>Preliminary data is later finalized.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NWS Local Storm Reports (LSRs)</td><td>Reports collected by the local NWS office (Brownsville/Rio Grande Valley for McAllen) from spotters, the public, law enforcement, and media during and right after storms.</td><td>Within minutes to hours.</td><td>Preliminary; may be corrected.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.spc.noaa.gov/climo/reports/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Prediction Center reports</a></td><td>National daily maps and lists of preliminary hail, wind, and tornado reports compiled from LSRs.</td><td>Same day or next day.</td><td>Preliminary; not quality-controlled.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NWS storm surveys</td><td>Damage surveys after significant wind or tornado events, with estimated wind speeds and damage descriptions.</td><td>Days after an event.</td><td>Often summarized later in NCEI narratives.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>NCEI Storm Events Database</h3>

<p>The National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) maintains the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Events Database</a>. It is the closest thing to an official, long-term record of hail, thunderstorm wind, tornadoes, flooding, freezes, and tropical storms in the United States. Each entry includes a begin date and time, a location, a magnitude, and often a narrative written by NWS staff.</p>

<p>Two things to keep in mind. First, it is updated monthly, and new data is typically published after a delay of a few months, so a storm from last week will not be there yet. Second, the most recent months may be marked preliminary until they are finalized. For older storms, NCEI is the best place to start.</p>

<h3>NWS Local Storm Reports</h3>

<p>The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/bro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NWS Brownsville/Rio Grande Valley office</a> serves McAllen and Hidalgo County. During severe weather, it collects reports of hail size, wind damage, flooding, and tornadoes from trained spotters, emergency managers, the public, and social media posts with photos. These Local Storm Reports are fast and specific, but preliminary. Sizes, times, and locations are sometimes corrected later.</p>

<h3>Storm Prediction Center reports</h3>

<p>The <a href="https://www.spc.noaa.gov/climo/reports/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SPC storm reports page</a> shows a national map and list of preliminary reports for each day. It is a quick way to confirm that hail or damaging wind was reported in the RGV on a given date. Because it draws from the same preliminary LSRs, treat it as a starting point rather than a final record.</p>

<h2>Real McAllen examples from the NOAA record</h2>

<p>Here is what NCEI entries look like for a few well-known Hidalgo County storms. Click through to see the full records.</p>

<h3>April 20, 2012: softball-size hail at McAllen High School</h3>

<p>The NCEI record for this event lists <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=379500" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4.5-inch hail reported by the public at McAllen High School</a>, described as softball size, along with golf ball to baseball size stones that fell for more than twenty minutes as the storm crossed the McAllen and Mission area. A related entry describes <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=379468" target="_blank" rel="noopener">golf ball to baseball size hail over a swath of the urban center of Mission and McAllen</a>. Notice how the narrative describes duration and path, not just a single size. That context can be useful when explaining why damage is widespread on a property.</p>

<h3>April 21, 2023: an hour of hail in southern Hidalgo County</h3>

<p>NCEI narratives describe up to an hour of continuous hail across southern Hidalgo County, with <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1084560" target="_blank" rel="noopener">golf ball to baseball size hail in the Peñitas area</a>, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1082244" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2.75-inch hail near McCook</a>, and <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1084558" target="_blank" rel="noopener">quarter-size hail covering yards in Mission with stones up to 2.5 inches</a>. Reports of about 1.25-inch hail were also logged in McAllen. Those differences show how hail size can vary widely within a few miles during the same storm.</p>

<h3>May 8, 2025: north McAllen supercell</h3>

<p>On May 8, 2025, a supercell produced <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1263450" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3-inch hail reported in north McAllen</a> (shared by a member of the public via Facebook), along with a <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1257702" target="_blank" rel="noopener">strong microburst with estimated gusts near 74 knots (about 85 mph)</a> near the McAllen–Edinburg city line. One storm, two types of damage: hail and straight-line wind. A claim may involve both.</p>

<h2>How to look up storms for your property</h2>

<p>You can search the NCEI database directly, but it takes some practice. We built three tools to make it easier for RGV property owners:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href="/storm-lookup/">Storm event lookup</a></strong> — search NOAA storm reports near a Hidalgo County city or ZIP code.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/storm-history/">Storm history</a></strong> — a filterable NOAA record of hail, wind, tornado, and flood events in the county.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/weather-events/">Recent weather events</a></strong> — automatically updated National Weather Service storm reports, useful for storms too recent to appear in NCEI.</li>
</ul>

<p>When you find a relevant report, write down or screenshot the event date and time, the location name, the magnitude, the source of the report, the link, and the date you retrieved it. That small habit makes it easy to share accurate information with an adjuster later.</p>

