<h2>How adjusters use date-of-loss data</h2>

<p>On hail and wind claims, insurers often check whether weather records support the reported date of loss. If the data shows a significant storm near the property on that date, the claim usually moves forward to inspection and scoping. If it does not, the adjuster may question the date, argue the damage is from an earlier storm, or deny the claim. Our article on <a href="/insurance-company-disputes-storm-damage/">what to do when an insurer disputes storm damage</a> covers those disputes in depth.</p>

<p>Some common ways date-of-loss data comes into play:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confirming the event.</strong> Did hail or damaging wind occur near the property on the claimed date?</li>
<li><strong>Checking hail size.</strong> Was reported hail large enough to plausibly damage the roofing material involved?</li>
<li><strong>Identifying prior storms.</strong> Were there earlier events, possibly before the policy started, that could explain the damage?</li>
<li><strong>Matching direction.</strong> Does the storm's movement fit the slopes and elevations that show damage?</li>
</ul>

<p>Insurers sometimes use commercial hail-mapping services that estimate hail size from radar. Those products can be useful, but they are estimates. If a report you receive conflicts with NOAA records or with what you saw, ask for a copy and compare.</p>

<h2>The limits of weather records</h2>

<p>Weather records are evidence, not proof. Being honest about their limits makes your claim more credible.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Nearby reports do not prove damage at your property</span><p>A 2.75-inch hail report two miles away does not mean your roof took 2.75-inch hail. Storm cores are narrow and hail size changes block by block. The physical condition of your property is what ultimately shows whether it was damaged.</p></div>

<ul>
<li><strong>Reporting gaps.</strong> Reports depend on someone measuring and reporting. Night storms and sparsely populated areas are underreported, so the absence of a report is not evidence that nothing happened.</li>
<li><strong>Location precision.</strong> Many reports are tied to a town name or approximate point, such as "Mc Allen" or "Shayland," not an exact address.</li>
<li><strong>Estimated values.</strong> Many wind speeds are estimated (EG) from damage rather than measured (MG) by an instrument.</li>
<li><strong>Preliminary data.</strong> LSRs and SPC reports may be revised, and NCEI data can change before it is finalized.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Building a stronger date-of-loss file</h2>

<p>Combine weather records with property-specific evidence. A practical file for a McAllen hail or wind claim might include:</p>
<ol>
<li>NOAA records for the claimed date (NCEI, LSR, or SPC), saved with links and retrieval dates.</li>
<li>Photos and videos from the day of the storm, especially of hailstones next to a ruler or coin.</li>
<li>Photos of collateral damage: dented gutters, vents, AC units, mailboxes, cars, and torn screens.</li>
<li>Roof and exterior photos taken soon after the storm, and any earlier photos showing prior condition.</li>
<li>Interior photos of new leaks or ceiling stains with dates.</li>
<li>Receipts for emergency repairs such as tarping.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> and guide to <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">documenting hail damage for an insurance claim</a> walk through each step.</p>

<h2>When a public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>A licensed Texas public adjuster can pull and interpret weather records, match them against the damage pattern on your property, and present both together in an organized claim. That is especially helpful when the insurer says no storm occurred, or blames an earlier event. If you are dealing with <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage</a> or <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage</a> in McAllen or elsewhere in Hidalgo County, a review of the weather record is often one of the first things we do. No adjuster can guarantee a result, but organized evidence tends to make conversations with the insurer more productive.</p>

